Q: How to Invoke IDBSet&lt;T&gt;.FirstOrDefault(predicate) using reflection? A: <p>It might be too later to party, but it is high possible to save couple hours of investigation for somebody in future. This code returns wrong FirstOrDefault method with boolean predicate. it returns method for getting collection and default value in case not found</p>
<pre><code>
 var selectMethod = typeof(Enumerable).GetMethods().First(m =&gt; m.Name == nameof(Enumerable.FirstOrDefault) &amp;&amp; m.GetParameters().Length == 2
 )
.MakeGenericMethod(type);
</code></pre>
<p>Because FirstOrdefault has two definitions with two parameters and select condition should be strictly</p>
<p>like this:</p>
<pre><code> var selectMethod = typeof(Enumerable).GetMethods().First(m =&gt; m.Name == nameof(Enumerable.FirstOrDefault) &amp;&amp; m.GetParameters().Length == 2 &amp;&amp;
 m.GetParameters()[1].ParameterType.IsGenericType &amp;&amp; m.GetParameters()[1].ParameterType.GetGenericTypeDefinition() == typeof(Func&lt;,&gt;)
 )
.MakeGenericMethod(type);
</code></pre>

Q: How to call completable future in a loop and combine all the results? A: <p>There is an issue with <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/46589510/how-to-call-completable-future-in-a-loop-and-combine-all-the-results/46856148#46856148">answer from Venkata Raju</a>. Raju used <strong><code>get</code></strong> call on future which is a blocking call and kills the main purpose of coding in Async style. Always avoid doing get on futures.</p>
<p>There are tons of built in methods build around handling future values like <code>thenApply</code>, <code>thenAccept</code>, <code>thenCompose</code>, <code>thenCombine</code>, etc.</p>
<p><code>CompletableFuture.allOf</code> method is meant to be used when you have to deal with multiple futures.</p>
<p>It has the following signature:</p>
<pre><code>public static CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt; allOf(CompletableFuture&lt;?&gt;... cfs)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Side note:</strong> <code>CompletableFuture.anyOf</code> can be used when you only care about the first future to complete. Use <code>allOf</code> when you need all futures to complete.</p>
<p>I would code your spec in following way using CompletableFuture.allOf.</p>
<pre><code>public class DorjeeTest {

    public static CompletableFuture&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt; getCompetableFutureResult() {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; getResult());
    }

    public static List&lt;String&gt; getResult() {
        return Lists.newArrayList(&quot;one&quot;, &quot;two&quot;, &quot;three&quot;);
    }

    public static void testFutures() {
        int x = 3;
        List&lt;CompletableFuture&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt;&gt; futureResultList = Lists.newArrayList();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; x; i++) {
            futureResultList.add(getCompetableFutureResult());
        }
        CompletableFuture[] futureResultArray = futureResultList.toArray(new CompletableFuture[futureResultList.size()]);
        CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt; combinedFuture = CompletableFuture.allOf(futureResultArray);
        CompletableFuture&lt;List&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt;&gt; finalResults = combinedFuture
                .thenApply(voidd -&gt;
                        futureResultList.stream()
                                .map(future -&gt; future.join())
                        .collect(Collectors.toList()));
        finalResults.thenAccept(result -&gt; System.out.println(result));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        testFutures();
        System.out.println(&quot;put debug break point on this line...&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to copy the source code of my website recursively into a zip file and download using PHP A: <p>I had the exact same problem some months ago and created a nice script for this:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>
&lt;?php

$zip = new ZipArchive();

$ret = $zip-&gt;open('archive.zip', ZipArchive::CREATE);
if ($ret === true) {
    addfilestozip($zip, '/path/to/dir');
    $zip-&gt;close();
} else {
    die('Error creating file!');
}


function addfilestozip($zip, $dir, $subdir = '', $exclude = [])
{
    $files = scandir(path($dir, $subdir));
    foreach ($files as $file) {
        if ($file == '.' || $file == '..') {
            continue;
        }
        if (in_array(path($subdir, $file), $exclude)) {
            continue;
        }
        if (is_dir(path($dir, $subdir, $file))) {
            addfilestozip($zip, $dir, path($subdir, $file), $exclude);
        }
        if (is_file(path($dir, $subdir, $file))) {
            $zip-&gt;addFile(path($dir, $subdir, $file), path($subdir, $file));
        }
    }
}

function path(...$p)
{
    $p = array_filter($p, fn ($e) =&gt; $e != '');

    return implode('/', $p);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Short explanation: You must do it file by file and recursively, that is the only way. That's why I use the recursive function <code>addfilestozip</code>. Unfortunately, you cannot just provide the file path to the zip as it then would save the whole path and not just the relative path from your starting point. The <code>path</code> function I'm using here makes correct relative paths.</p>
<p>Hope this helps. You probably need to modify it to your needs.</p>
